WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.870
I think an important thing in any learning
strategy or any learning method that

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.800
we use is to do what we want to do.

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:12.240
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here,
uh, today I want to talk about

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:15.210
my strategy for learning Arabic.

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:19.470
And remember if you enjoy my
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:20.520
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:21.314 --> 00:00:25.575
And if you follow me, follow me on a
podcast service, please leave a review.

00:00:26.145 --> 00:00:32.744
So Arabic, Arabic spoken by, I don't
know, 350-400 million people in the world.

00:00:32.805 --> 00:00:34.574
An area of the world
that's often in the news.

00:00:35.324 --> 00:00:38.385
Well, I was very motivated to
learn Arabic, but I've been at

00:00:38.385 --> 00:00:42.375
it for quite a while and I'm
probably not doing that well.

00:00:42.945 --> 00:00:47.864
And so I want to go over my strategy
and things that, uh, maybe have changed

00:00:47.864 --> 00:00:51.825
in my strategy and maybe my experience
can be helpful to other people.

00:00:52.305 --> 00:00:56.175
Uh, I will show you in my statistics
at LingQ when I started with

00:00:56.175 --> 00:01:01.245
Arabic, uh, I will show you that
I started Arabic in April of 2018.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:08.040
That not long thereafter I also started
learning Persian and not long after

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.660
that I started learning Turkish.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:13.280
So I had this strategy of learning three
major languages of the Middle East.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:15.545
Uh, my motivation was...

00:01:15.615 --> 00:01:18.315
I started with Hebrew cause
my wife and I were visiting...

00:01:18.315 --> 00:01:22.245
in fact, I did Greek and Hebrew cause
we were going to visit Crete and Israel.

00:01:22.525 --> 00:01:26.475
Then I went to Jordan and I said,
there's a lot more Arabic speakers.

00:01:26.475 --> 00:01:28.815
So I went and decided to learn Arabic.

00:01:29.185 --> 00:01:31.905
Then I came home and discovered
that in Vancouver, we have so

00:01:31.905 --> 00:01:34.155
many Iranian immigrants here.

00:01:34.155 --> 00:01:36.585
So I decided to learn
Persian at the same time.

00:01:37.095 --> 00:01:42.120
Uh, and then my wife was watching this
Turkish mini-series on Netflix so I

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.720
said, why don't I go after these three
major languages of the Middle East?

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:51.390
Um, I dropped Turkish, uh, because
written in the Latin alphabet,

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:52.890
it's in fact easier for me.

00:01:53.685 --> 00:01:58.005
A major obstacle in my Arabic
learning is the writing system.

00:01:58.005 --> 00:02:02.745
It's simply so difficult for the brain to
get used to a different writing system.

00:02:03.075 --> 00:02:06.255
So I decided to focus on
Persian and Arabic, both of

00:02:06.255 --> 00:02:08.145
which use the Arabic script.

00:02:08.175 --> 00:02:13.335
Then the other problem, of course,
with Arabic there is standard Arabic,

00:02:13.335 --> 00:02:18.855
which nobody speaks as a native
language, but which is the language

00:02:18.855 --> 00:02:23.715
used in, you know, education in, in
political discussions, on, you know,

00:02:23.745 --> 00:02:25.845
television, podcasts and so forth.

00:02:26.325 --> 00:02:31.245
Uh, most things are written in Standard
Arabic, but if you go to Lebanon or

00:02:31.245 --> 00:02:37.450
Syria or Egypt, not to mention Morocco
or Iraq or the Persian Gulf uh,

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.090
people speak various forms of Arabic.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:47.290
And so I initially had the strategy of
focusing my Arabic, uh, effort on Standard

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:52.540
Arabic, but then more recently I decided
because the movies are all from Lebanon

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.235
or from Egypt or sometimes from the Gulf.

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:58.895
Uh, and of course that's the
language that people speak that

00:02:58.895 --> 00:03:00.425
is the conversational language.

00:03:00.425 --> 00:03:02.825
So I said, I should try
to learn those as well.

00:03:03.215 --> 00:03:09.355
So I had a tutor in, uh, Egyptian
Arabic, in fact, yeah one, and we  have

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:14.795
at LingQ, we have a number of resources
in both Levantine and Egyptian Arabic.

00:03:15.665 --> 00:03:20.735
Uh, but what I found after a while is that
by trying to speak Egyptian Arabic with

00:03:20.735 --> 00:03:25.290
my Egyptian Arabic tutor, in the end, I
ended up not being able to speak anything.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.130
Uh, and so I had this experience
the other day, and this is what sort

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:32.610
of triggered my decision to change
my strategy, which I'm coming to.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:37.770
Um, so I was actually cleaning
my deck because my deck, I have a

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:42.570
big wooden deck and get a lot of,
uh, you know, moss growing on it.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.310
So I actually bought a power
washer and a great power washer.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.380
It took me quite a while to
assemble it, but it works.

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:53.940
Uh, and in the middle of doing that,
I said, I'm going to go down...

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.609
I wasn't confident the power
washer would do the job.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.730
I'm going to buy some vinegar
and I'm going to scrub the

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.110
deck as well with vinegar.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:02.730
And then I said, I'll drop by...

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:07.079
there's an area where there's
like three Persian, uh, stores.

00:04:07.079 --> 00:04:11.609
One is a guy who fixes electronic
equipment and then there's a little

00:04:11.609 --> 00:04:14.880
place where they sell kebabs and then
there's a coffee shop and there's a

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.039
little patio in between the three of them.

00:04:17.039 --> 00:04:17.700
So I go there.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:22.950
So I walk into the coffee shop and I, and
it's got the same name as the kebab shop.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.419
And I said, are you connected?

00:04:24.419 --> 00:04:24.789
Yes.

00:04:24.789 --> 00:04:25.599
So I can eat here?

00:04:25.599 --> 00:04:26.289
Yes.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.950
And then I said, Salaam expecting to
speak Persian, but she was from Lebanon.

00:04:32.099 --> 00:04:36.359
So we spoke Arabic and I couldn't
say anything in Levantine Arabic.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.989
Basically what came out
was my Standard Arabic.

00:04:40.619 --> 00:04:42.510
And she was very happy with my Arabic.

00:04:42.539 --> 00:04:45.479
She told other people, of course,
they're always very nice to you about

00:04:45.479 --> 00:04:49.080
how well I speak Arabic, which isn't
true, of course, but I was surprised

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.919
at how much I could say in Standard
Arabic, even though I had left it and

00:04:52.919 --> 00:04:54.840
was trying to learn Egyptian Arabic.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:02.430
Just as a matter of interest, uh, people
who walked into that, oh, and this lady

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:06.180
who was originally from Lebanon had lived
in China, but she didn't speak Chinese.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:07.260
She'd lived in Sweden.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.950
So we spok in Swedish and she had some
connection with Russian, which I don't

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:14.820
know, but she spoke Russian and lo and
behold, some Persian people came in and

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:19.920
we spoke Persian and then another person
came in from Moldova and we spoke Russian.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:21.450
So I had a whole...

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:21.630
oh.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:24.045
And there was another gentleman
sitting there who had lived in

00:05:24.045 --> 00:05:25.545
Korea and wanted to learn languages.

00:05:25.545 --> 00:05:27.015
And I persuaded him to join LingQ.

00:05:27.284 --> 00:05:31.695
I had a great time, but I walked away
from that saying the language, the Arabic

00:05:31.695 --> 00:05:35.655
language that I spent the most time with,
the Arabic language that I can actually

00:05:35.655 --> 00:05:38.395
say something in is Standard Arabic.

00:05:38.875 --> 00:05:44.655
I now know enough of Egyptian Arabic
and Levantine Arabic that some of

00:05:44.655 --> 00:05:46.605
the things you know, that they do...

00:05:46.605 --> 00:05:54.164
so it's, you know ... uh, you know, the
Levantine's say ... at the end of the

00:05:54.164 --> 00:05:59.205
sentence instead of ... at the beginning
to introduce a question, uh, you know

00:06:01.405 --> 00:06:06.495
... or all these things in Egyptian, uh, or in
Levantine, I know enough of them now that

00:06:06.495 --> 00:06:12.375
I can understand better if someone is kind
of speaking mostly ... but mixing in a lot

00:06:12.375 --> 00:06:15.315
of dialect or a lot of regional variation.

00:06:15.315 --> 00:06:18.615
So I've kind of achieved what I hope
to achieve, but I'm going to go back

00:06:18.615 --> 00:06:24.165
to speaking Standard Arabic mixing in a
bit of Egyptian or Levantine, depending

00:06:24.165 --> 00:06:26.445
on the situation, hoping to understand.

00:06:26.595 --> 00:06:28.935
And very often we pick
up from other people.

00:06:28.935 --> 00:06:32.985
So if I'm speaking to someone and I'm
sort of mixing in, it's a bit like

00:06:32.985 --> 00:06:38.760
my ... if I mix in a bit of Egyptian
or Levantine but stick to what I know.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.219
I think that's going to
enable me to communicate.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:45.230
So my strategy where I've said,
initially, I said, you know, stay

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:47.789
with ... because I can listen to podcasts.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:52.859
Then I said, no, I'm going to learn,
you know, Egyptian or Levantine and

00:06:52.919 --> 00:06:54.479
put some effort into both of those.

00:06:54.989 --> 00:07:00.299
And now I'm saying those are good for
comprehension and I'll mix some of

00:07:00.299 --> 00:07:05.700
those expressions in, but basically
I'm going to stay with, um, Standard.

00:07:07.365 --> 00:07:12.135
So I think an important thing in any
learning strategy or any learning method

00:07:12.135 --> 00:07:16.215
that we use is to do what we want to do.

00:07:16.844 --> 00:07:19.635
The only thing that really matters in
language learning is the attitude of

00:07:19.635 --> 00:07:21.465
the learner and the time we put in.

00:07:22.125 --> 00:07:25.545
So some people like flashcards,
some people like Anki, some

00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:27.825
people like a Duolingo.

00:07:27.825 --> 00:07:28.605
It doesn't matter.

00:07:28.755 --> 00:07:32.805
As long as you enjoy what you're doing,
you will improve as long as you're

00:07:32.805 --> 00:07:34.695
putting in the time, you'll do fine.

00:07:35.135 --> 00:07:37.950
So on that basis and, and we can change.

00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:39.490
I can think that my...

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:40.979
I should be doing this in Arabic.

00:07:40.979 --> 00:07:41.969
And then I changed my mind.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:45.630
The other thing I would do now
is I would not attempt three

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:46.770
languages at the same time.

00:07:47.835 --> 00:07:51.765
Uh, because it means you're going to
be slowed down in each one of them.

00:07:52.245 --> 00:07:54.974
On the other hand, I
have a sense of Persian.

00:07:55.034 --> 00:07:56.505
I can actually converse in person.

00:07:56.685 --> 00:08:01.544
I can kind of converse in Arabic
and I have a bit of a sense of what

00:08:01.544 --> 00:08:07.635
Turkish is all about and whatever I
put into those languages it's not lost.

00:08:07.755 --> 00:08:11.325
Cause if I go back to my Turkish, which
is the, now the weakest of the three,

00:08:11.595 --> 00:08:16.275
very quickly will I get back to where
I was, and then I can take it forward.

00:08:16.485 --> 00:08:22.455
So it's not a, I didn't make a mistake
by sort of dissipating my energies.

00:08:22.605 --> 00:08:26.925
It just meant that I didn't get as far
in any one of them as I would have been

00:08:26.925 --> 00:08:28.905
able to do had I focused on just one.

00:08:29.325 --> 00:08:32.865
So, and I'm going to leave you with
a little walkthrough of my statistics

00:08:32.865 --> 00:08:38.085
at LingQ so that you'll see, you know,
what the history has been of my, uh,

00:08:38.115 --> 00:08:41.085
learning, uh, of those of those languages.

00:08:42.075 --> 00:08:47.415
And after that, uh, I will leave a
couple of videos that you can go to,

00:08:48.045 --> 00:08:53.895
uh, to see, you know, my Arabic, uh,
three years ago, and then two years ago,

00:08:54.105 --> 00:08:55.695
already, four years ago and two years ago.

00:08:56.055 --> 00:08:56.565
Okay.

00:08:56.595 --> 00:08:57.405
Thank you for listening.

00:08:57.405 --> 00:08:57.975
Bye for now.

00:08:58.385 --> 00:09:01.045
I thought I'd have a look at
what I've been doing in Arabic.

00:09:01.645 --> 00:09:06.420
This is my Arabic page at LingQ,
as it looks right now, I've

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:07.800
kind of been bouncing around.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:11.490
I've been importing some articles.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:14.520
I've been looking at
my Jordanian cartoons.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:17.790
I watched a bit of this Egyptian movie.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:21.390
Then I go back to my
mini-stories in Standard Arabic.

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:25.740
I've also done my mini-stories in
Egyptian Arabic, a little bit in...

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:29.040
it doesn't show here, but
in Levantine Arabic as well.

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:36.750
And, uh, I'm kind of a bit at loose
ends here as I've explained in my video,

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:39.600
but let's just look at my history.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:44.130
So I think, you know that if you click
on your, or you can even, you know,

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:48.150
do it this way, hit the down arrow,
you've got to get to your profile and

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:50.700
you can see what your history has been.

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:54.825
So you can see that I haven't been that
active this, these past seven days.

00:09:55.035 --> 00:10:00.105
However, if I go to my all time
statistics, I can see that I

00:10:00.105 --> 00:10:04.995
started poking around at Arabic,
uh, around the end of 2017.

00:10:04.995 --> 00:10:09.595
If I look at known words, if I look
at known words, if I look at LingQs

00:10:09.615 --> 00:10:17.805
created, I actually started, I guess,
you know, poking around in 2017, but

00:10:17.805 --> 00:10:24.525
really my major effort was early in
2018, that was a major spurt in Arabic.

00:10:25.275 --> 00:10:30.765
Um, and I've gone through phases, as I
said, between my major initial effort,

00:10:30.824 --> 00:10:35.535
effort in ... and more recently trying
to get a, a, a bit of an exposure to,

00:10:35.594 --> 00:10:37.364
uh, Levantine and Egyptian Arabic.

00:10:37.814 --> 00:10:40.545
Now you'll see this date, 2018.

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:43.170
2018 April.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:48.750
If I look at my situation in, first of
all, I see the first Semitic language I

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:53.880
started with was Hebrew, uh, because I
was going to, to Israel and I put some

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:58.500
effort into Hebrew, but when my wife and
I ended up going to Jordan and I realized

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:03.210
that actually there's a lot more Arabic
speakers than I dropped Hebrew entirely.

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:03.810
I may go back.

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:10.080
But, uh, so I started into my, uh,
Arabic, and then I came back to

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.890
Vancouver and lo and behold, there's
far more Persian speakers here.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:22.140
So again here by about, you know, the, I
guess the beginning of 2019, I was putting

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.080
more effort into Persian than into Arabic.

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:30.600
And so I've kind of kept my Persian
activities going alongside my Arabic.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:34.740
If I go back to where my Arabic was
you'll see that I've kind of kept

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:36.840
them both going, going simultaneously.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:43.200
The other thing that I did was I decided
to, you know, I'm interested in the

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:48.540
Middle East and my wife is watching
series on Netflix in Turkey, Turkish.

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:54.510
So by the middle of 2019, I put a three
month stint into learning Turkish.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:12:00.645
So a lot of distractions
from my Arabic learning.

00:12:01.064 --> 00:12:05.954
Uh, so I just wanted to show you that
as a sort of, um, some background to,

00:12:06.375 --> 00:12:12.944
uh, what I, uh, was talking about in
my, uh, video on my Arabic learning

00:12:12.944 --> 00:12:18.765
strategy and, and, uh, you know, I
will leave you with two videos that

00:12:18.765 --> 00:12:24.345
you can watch to see how well I did
at two different stages in my Arabic.

